---
// src: Astro Crash Course: https://youtube.com/playlist?list=PL4cUxeGkcC9hXL99yOeCcUrdUee8u36s3&si=mRLs-awoP1bJiR4F
// Frontmatter: plain JavaScript comments
// a way to add metadata to a file
// utilized  within components to add component scripts
// type javascript or typescript code
// setup variables to use in a component
// fetch data from a database
// access component props
// never runs in the browser
// astro components are rendered at buildtime or on-demand on the server. Not in the browser.
// none of the code written here will ever reach the browser
// you can configure a component to render on-demand
// variables written here can be accessed inside of components
// to output variables, use {} inside of a component
// use server side renderings (ssr) to pull user data for display in a component
// when a request for the data comes to the server, you can fetch the currently authenticated user info

// Content collections help you mnage and query data for your site
// blog posts, page conent, etc..
// you can add this data locally via JSON or MD files, or you can fetch it from a remote source like a database or api endpoint
// by using astros content collections, you can easily query and load data into components
// you can also add schemas for collections which enforce type safety and add intellisense
// A schema is a set of rules for the shape your data must have: which fields exist, what type each one is, and which are required. When data doesn’t match, you get an error.
// In Astro, schemas come up in content collections, folders of Markdown or JSON files such as book entries. You define the schema in the content config file using Zod, a validation library built into Astro
// when you use a json file as a data source for a content collection, each object needs an id to identify it

// import components
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";

// server rendering simulation by randomizing whether this user value is an object or null (not logged-in)
const getUser = () => {
  // generate a random number between 0 and 1
  // if that number is less than 0.5, return null
  // otherwise return the user object
  // randomizes what value you get back each time this runs
  // runs once at build time
  // this code will run everytime a user refreshes the page
  //
  return Math.random() < 0.5
    ? null
    : {
        name: "Adrian",
        email: "adrian@bookbites.com",
      };
};

const user = getUser();
---

<BaseLayout>
  {/* Stripped from the build. Use this for notes to yourself */}
  <!-- Kept in the HTML. Anyone can see this in the page source -->
  {/* this fills the slot in the BaseLayout file */}
  <div class="content">
    <!-- if a user is null, you can access the other properties -->
    <!-- in order for it to work, you have to wrap this output in some conditional logic -->
    <!-- to only render this user info IF there is a value for the user -->
    <!-- checks if you have a user, if you do, user info renders, if not, noting renders -->
    {user && (
      <>
        <h2>Welcome, {user.name}</h2>
        <p>Logged in as: {user.email}</p>
      </>
    )}

    <p>
      Lorem ipsum dolor sit amet consectetur adipisicing elit. Dignissimos,
      quas!
    </p>
    <a href="/books">Take a look at my reviews</a>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  /* CSS comment */
  .content {
    text-align: center;
  }
  a {
    display: inline-block;
    background-color: #7a49c5;
    color: #fff;
    padding: 6px 10px;
    border-radius: 2px;
    text-decoration: none;
    margin-top: 20px;
  }
  a:hover {
    background-color: #965ee9;
  }
</style>
